\section{Introduction and Ontological Foundations}
\subsection{The Primacy of Worldline Geometry}
The Scalar-Angular-Torsion (SAT) framework proposes that four-dimensional superhelical 
worldlines ($X_i$) are the primary mathematical entities of physical reality. Unlike traditional 
Minkowski space treatments where worldlines are representational histories, SAT asserts that 
matter, mass, and interactions are emergent properties of the worldline’s intrinsic curvature ($
\kappa$) and torsion ($\tau$). By parameterizing worldlines solely by arc-length ($\lambda$), 
the framework establishes a purely geometric universe.
\subsection{Metric Foundation and Lorentzian Recovery}
The manifold is defined as a purely spacelike Euclidean 4-space ($\mathbb{R}^4$) with a 
metric signature of $(+,+,+,+)$. Observed Lorentzian phenomena—including light cones and 
causality—emerge from a preferred expansion vector field ($\vec{T}$). This field describes a 
uniform, radial expansion from all points in 3-space relative to the 4D bulk, defined by the 
expansion factor $r(\lambda) = ct$.


## Page 3

Standard Minkowski metrics are recovered in the limit where local worldline velocities ($v$) 
approach the critical velocity threshold
\begin{equation}
v_{\text{crit}} = B \cdot c \approx 0.2387c,
\end{equation}
where $B$ is the Projection Constant.
\section{Formalism: Analytical Worldline Geometry}
\subsection{Recursive Superhelical Parameterization}
To ensure differentiability of the worldline and stability of high-order derivatives, the path 
$X_i(\lambda)$ is defined as a 4D superhelix ($\mathbf{H}$) winding along a notional $S^3$ 
hypersphere coordinate system . A recursive harmonic interleaving stabilizes the fourth-order 
torsion ($\tau$), constructing the position vector as:
\begin{widetext}
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{H}(\lambda) = \begin{pmatrix} R(\lambda) \cos(\omega_s \lambda + \phi_0) \\ 
R(\lambda) \sin(\omega_s \lambda + \phi_0) \\ r_h \cos(\omega_h \lambda + \psi_0) \\ r_h 
\sin(\omega_h \lambda + \psi_0) \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
\end{widetext}
Here $R(\lambda)$ represents the macroscopic expansion scale and $r_h$ denotes the 
superhelical radius. This construction prevents curvature spikes by ensuring convergence of 
higher-order derivatives.
\subsection{Total SAT Lagrangian and Coefficient Mapping}
The ontological coefficients $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma)$ are mapped to mechanical properties of 
the 4D manifold:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Filament Stiffness ($\kappa$):} $\kappa \approx m_0 c^2 \ell_f$


